\section*{Lech Multiplicity Conjecture for Flat Local Extensions}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For a flat local map of nonzero Noetherian rings, the proposed inequality is $e(R)\leq e(S)$ without a dimension or characteristic restriction.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For any flat local homomorphism \((R,\mathfrak m)\to(S,\mathfrak n)\) between nonzero Noetherian local rings, the claimed Hilbert--Samuel multiplicities satisfy
\[
 e_{\mathfrak m}(R)\le e_{\mathfrak n}(S).
\]
The theorem imposes no extra condition on residue fields, dimensions, or characteristic.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The argument replaces unavailable Ulrich modules by short free complexes and a uniform estimate for their Euler lengths. Cohen factorization connects those estimates to multiplicity, while normalized-length algebras provide the parameter calculations in positive and mixed characteristic.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Theorem 1.1 proves exactly the all-flat-local Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity comparison, without imposing a characteristic, ramification, dimension, or domain restriction. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 194, the Lean entry concerns positive-characteristic complete-domain Dutta multiplicity and its Frobenius limit; arbitrary-characteristic flat-local monotonicity is beyond that entry. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{DuttaDomain.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

